### What are RPO and RTO?

RPO answers how much data you can lose. If your RPO is five minutes, you must recover to a state no more than five minutes before the disaster, which demands frequent or continuous replication. RTO answers how long you can be down. If your RTO is ten minutes, the whole failover must complete within ten minutes. Tighter objectives cost more.

```java
// DR targets drive the strategy choice
package com.bank.infrastructure;

public class DrTargets {
    private final String workload;
    private final int rpoMinutes;
    private final int rtoMinutes;

    public DrTargets(String workload, int rpoMinutes, int rtoMinutes) {
        this.workload = workload;
        this.rpoMinutes = rpoMinutes;
        this.rtoMinutes = rtoMinutes;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        DrTargets ledger = new DrTargets("Core Ledger", 1, 5);
        DrTargets api = new DrTargets("Customer API", 15, 30);
        DrTargets reports = new DrTargets("Internal Reports", 1440, 240);
        System.out.println(ledger.workload + ": RPO=" + ledger.rpoMinutes + "m, RTO=" + ledger.rtoMinutes + "m");
        System.out.println(api.workload + ": RPO=" + api.rpoMinutes + "m, RTO=" + api.rtoMinutes + "m");
        System.out.println(reports.workload + ": RPO=" + reports.rpoMinutes + "m, RTO=" + reports.rtoMinutes + "m");
    }
}
```

### What is Backup and Restore?

Backup and Restore is the cheapest strategy. You take regular backups -- EBS snapshots, RDS snapshots, S3 cross-region copies -- and on disaster you provision infrastructure from infrastructure-as-code and restore from backup. RPO equals your backup frequency and RTO is hours because you rebuild from nothing.

```java
// Backup and Restore strategy
package com.bank.infrastructure;

public class BackupRestoreStrategy {
    public static void explain() {
        System.out.println("Backup and Restore:");
        System.out.println("  Daily backups to another Region (S3, RDS snapshots)");
        System.out.println("  On disaster: deploy IaC -> restore backups -> start app");
        System.out.println("  RPO: hours (= backup frequency)");
        System.out.println("  RTO: hours (rebuild from scratch)");
        System.out.println("  Cost: lowest (only backup storage)");
        System.out.println("  Suitable for: internal reporting, non-critical workloads");
    }
}
```

### What is Pilot Light?

Pilot Light keeps the critical core always running in the recovery Region, typically a continuously replicated database. The application tier exists only as configuration or stopped resources. On disaster you start the application tier, scale it up, and point traffic over. Because the data is already there, RPO is small.

```java
// Pilot Light strategy
package com.bank.infrastructure;

public class PilotLightStrategy {
    public static void explain() {
        System.out.println("Pilot Light:");
        System.out.println("  Replicated database running in DR Region (continuous)");
        System.out.println("  App tier: AMIs/containers ready but NOT running");
        System.out.println("  On disaster: start app tier -> scale up -> Route 53 failover");
        System.out.println("  RPO: seconds to minutes (continuous replication)");
        System.out.println("  RTO: tens of minutes (start and scale app)");
        System.out.println("  Cost: moderate (replicated DB + dormant app config)");
    }
}
```

### What is Warm Standby?

Warm Standby runs a scaled-down but fully functional copy of the entire stack in the recovery Region all the time. On disaster you scale it up to full capacity and shift traffic. The system is already running and just needs to grow.

```java
// Warm Standby strategy
package com.bank.infrastructure;

public class WarmStandbyStrategy {
    public static void explain() {
        System.out.println("Warm Standby:");
        System.out.println("  Scaled-down copy running in DR Region (fully functional)");
        System.out.println("  Replicated database + running app at reduced capacity");
        System.out.println("  On disaster: scale up to full -> Route 53 failover");
        System.out.println("  RPO: small (continuous replication)");
        System.out.println("  RTO: minutes (already running, just scale)");
        System.out.println("  Cost: higher (always-on standby)");
    }
}
```

### What is Multi-Site Active-Active?

Multi-Site Active-Active runs full capacity in two or more Regions, all serving live traffic, with data replicated both ways or a globally distributed database. If a Region fails, the others absorb its traffic almost instantly. RPO and RTO are near zero. It is the most expensive and complex.

```java
// Multi-Site Active-Active strategy
package com.bank.infrastructure;

public class ActiveActiveStrategy {
    public static void explain() {
        System.out.println("Multi-Site Active-Active:");
        System.out.println("  Full capacity in 2+ Regions, all serving live traffic");
        System.out.println("  Data: bidirectional replication or global database");
        System.out.println("  On Region failure: others absorb traffic instantly");
        System.out.println("  RPO: near zero");
        System.out.println("  RTO: near zero (seconds)");
        System.out.println("  Cost: highest (full infra in each Region)");
        System.out.println("  Complexity: data consistency across Regions");
    }
}
```

### How do you choose a strategy per workload?

You do not pick one strategy for the whole company. You pick per workload based on business criticality. The core payment ledger might justify Active-Active, while an internal analytics dashboard is fine on Backup and Restore.

| Strategy | RPO | RTO | Cost | Best For |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Backup and Restore | Hours | Hours | Lowest | Internal, non-critical |
| Pilot Light | Minutes | Tens of min | Moderate | Important services |
| Warm Standby | Small | Minutes | Higher | Customer-facing |
| Active-Active | Near zero | Near zero | Highest | Core banking, payments |

```java
// Strategy selector based on RPO/RTO targets
package com.bank.infrastructure;

public class DrStrategySelector {
    public static String select(int rpoMinutes, int rtoMinutes) {
        if (rpoMinutes <= 1 && rtoMinutes <= 5)    return "Multi-Site Active-Active";
        if (rpoMinutes <= 15 && rtoMinutes <= 30)   return "Warm Standby";
        if (rpoMinutes <= 60 && rtoMinutes <= 60)   return "Pilot Light";
        return "Backup and Restore";
    }
}
```

### Why must you test DR regularly?

A DR plan is worthless unless you test the failover regularly, because untested recovery procedures fail exactly when you need them. Game-day exercises validate that the RTO and RPO are actually achievable, that runbooks work, and that the team knows what to do under pressure.

```java
// DR testing schedule
package com.bank.infrastructure;

public class DrTestingSchedule {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("DR Testing Schedule for NBI:");
        System.out.println("  Quarterly: game-day failover drill for core ledger");
        System.out.println("  Monthly: backup restoration test for reporting DB");
        System.out.println("  Annually: full Region failover exercise");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("After each test:");
        System.out.println("  1. Record actual RPO and RTO achieved");
        System.out.println("  2. Document gaps and failures");
        System.out.println("  3. Update runbooks and automation");
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Senior engineers know that the four strategies are not mutually exclusive within an organization. They pick per workload, they automate failover with Route 53 health checks and infrastructure-as-code, and they track RPO and RTO as measurable SLIs. They also know that cross-Region data replication introduces consistency challenges that must be designed for, not discovered during an incident.
